#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049008 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297 :topic/math-academy-id 147 :topic/title "Multiplying Square Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825 :topic/math-academy-id 1195 :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector"}] :topic/id #uuid "d30fd98e-7cc3-5d03-98e7-14f311f99f96" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100696 :question/id #uuid "f7868f7f-b296-5a38-9af6-61dc4d1198a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4408" :question/problem "Calculate the product $I_3 B$ given that $I_3$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix and\n![](images/9b/9b8d8f439845a4949789fe313997d2245249e1a4df58c750d7cda0af36f1ce18.png)" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $I_3$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n![](images/ac/ac591cf90fae6e8275fed52c26c8487bff9d654733d414974631948fe30b8150.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100697 :question/id #uuid "9832238f-baff-5527-a11a-c65b6529cb0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4410" :question/problem "Calculate the product $C D,$ if\n![](images/94/945664c408577aac1d1a745120b3c17c46fd9db93e6d26cc53a4e501390936e2.png)\nand \n![](images/90/907ffa0b29d96249fe03a7fce3fe682c08982b14c7b7f72840540e6269196425.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3,$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $C.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/98/980ae75452824b4a8aa701b4e11be8c31448557933357458329f0a2b73a1cca6.png)\n\nDon't believe it? Let's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ -1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1171 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048452 :topic/math-academy-id 2087 :topic/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Distinct Eigenvalues"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050122 :topic/math-academy-id 1724 :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication"}] :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049125 :topic/math-academy-id 863 :topic/title "Introduction to the Inverse of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446 :topic/math-academy-id 1725 :topic/title "Powers of Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727 :topic/math-academy-id 232 :topic/title "The Transpose of a Matrix"}] :topic/id #uuid "268943d4-aaa4-5e39-9db5-48d7523acdc3" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161771 :question/id #uuid "c68d70a2-e4f8-5f08-8220-3df420a1e199" :question/math-academy-id "e-8156" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](images/fb/fb428c39bbc03be798bca2463658676de5c684396001cf547e9395352f456bc9.svg)" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](images/3f/3f91ec40e717eac93bb38f9c13f7b1700e84ac109ccceaffbb8473ba8d6b4e34.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161816 :question/id #uuid "b159d52a-a436-5743-9151-2e147364a41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8478" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\nIII. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/81/812e9888b920e0338fd72116464b8b96c8c272f95f180cd46aa3f1db9afac8d2.svg)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3118 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493 :topic/math-academy-id 3335 :topic/title "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048859 :topic/math-academy-id 2824 :topic/title "The Second Derivative of a Multivariable Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049450 :topic/math-academy-id 3123 :topic/title "Quadratic Forms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049489 :topic/math-academy-id 2125 :topic/title "Projection Matrices, Linear Transformations and Their Properties"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050066 :topic/math-academy-id 2757 :topic/title "Adjacency Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050837 :topic/math-academy-id 1392 :topic/title "Distance Matrices"}] :topic/title "Symmetric Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050122 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049008 :topic/math-academy-id 1171 :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407 :topic/math-academy-id 1196 :topic/title "Multiplying Matrices"}] :topic/id #uuid "2eaec2b9-4c03-5efd-b5aa-8e9655cca4f4" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115056 :question/id #uuid "32bd6b27-e95d-5de4-941f-309eb0bc61f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4411" :question/problem "Find $AB-CB,$ where\n![](images/b4/b4b5ccc4e5075ba81dc840743f0267ba2e5f28d75a5be09de54df111203b949a.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB-CB=(A-C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A-C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-C & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB-CB & =(A-C)B \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =-B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3} & 45 \\\\ -81 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Distributive Law"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115227 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115057 :question/id #uuid "3b1395b6-49c1-5e0d-ac94-c2d6fc242f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4412" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC,$ where\n![](images/32/32c0a3389207452c52376ce4305109e1c5ed634a07d2c99771825c39b5482940.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC = (AB)C = A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 1$ matrix that will require finding only $3$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115058 :question/id #uuid "7ab158e6-4222-5cab-88a0-85ce74a7d4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4413" :question/problem "Given that $A, B,$ and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n\nII. $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n\nIII. $A(B+C) = AB+AC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\nAB + AC  \\neq BA + CA.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1724 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446 :topic/math-academy-id 1725 :topic/title "Powers of Matrices"}] :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050446 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050122 :topic/math-academy-id 1724 :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication"}] :topic/id #uuid "49ac363b-d07a-55f5-9f5d-8e58eafc76b5" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115059 :question/id #uuid "f832e671-b4bd-5d1d-81e7-b215a8ee4e27" :question/math-academy-id "e-4414" :question/problem "Find $A^4$ given that ![](images/e0/e0e7d5ed9b893352d68931ff5d8fa93f0d0c5173e5955a3f5cdc0cb65d3c3c78.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, to find $A^2,$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A^4 = A^2 \\cdot A^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}81 & 0 \\\\ 100 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115230 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115060 :question/id #uuid "bea8819f-6feb-5de6-a585-4379378ccdbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4417" :question/problem "Consider the matrix\n![](images/f5/f5b9dd39e03e7a8c984d6a38db8dc32326669d134ff0b5aa012c421308b48351.svg)\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & =-{X}^{2}+3X+2 \\\\  & =-{X}^{2}+3X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)=-D^2+3D+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate $D^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & =-{D}^{2}+3D+2I \\\\  & =-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 15 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3+2 & 10+15+0 \\\\ 0 & -9-9+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 25 \\\\ 0 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1725 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048726 :topic/math-academy-id 1972 :topic/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048869 :topic/math-academy-id 781 :topic/title "Proving Matrix Identities Using Induction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049678 :topic/math-academy-id 1969 :topic/title "Properties of Diagonalization"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/math-academy-id 3118 :topic/title "Symmetric Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050066 :topic/math-academy-id 2757 :topic/title "Adjacency Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050492 :topic/math-academy-id 3776 :topic/title "Nilpotent and Idempotent Matrices"}] :topic/title "Powers of Matrices"}]}
